  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217432/assets/a0eb36fd37d73ed20e0ee17717f80cb33a56910cc57eb181897ad44197a7f268.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217432/assets/2b4771270aec2dd62828f4063262cfaf81fba31f6be6d1a2ba8bfe0d59778b48.png)\n\nThe point $(3,4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-1=3.$",
